From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Tue, 22 Nov 1994 17:02:36 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-11-323] GENE: Resurrecting the dodo?

I have thought about the "Jurassic Park scenario" lately, where DNA from
an extinct species is recreated, multiplied by PCR, inserted into living
eggs and hatched. While the possibilities of recreating dinosaurs
currently are rather remote, the idea might work for extinct species we
have recent DNA from. So, what species might be good candidates for a
resurrecting experiment? 

My favorite is the dodo, for several reasons. There are without doubt
preserved DNA from it, mainly in the form of stuffed individuals,
skeletons and perhaps some empty eggs stored at various museums. Since 
the dodo is a bird and have some living relatives, cloning the DNA and 
inserting it into a bird egg seems much simpler than the prospect for 
more distant animals, such as mammals or dinosaurs. 

Other reasons to attempt to resurrect the dodo are memetic. It would not
only provide an interesting technical challenge, it would also definitely
grip the public imagination. It would demonstrate our power not only to
drive animals to extinction, but also to bring them back and perhaps
implant them into their original environment (which might be a good idea,
since the dodo apparently was needed for the spread of some species of
trees on Mauritius). This would probably show that genetic engineering
doesn't have to be destructive, but can be used for environmentally
friendly purposes and repair damage to the environment. Besides, the dodo
is fairly cute, so it might be a good idea to invest into some
merchandising. After all, if the ET dolls could sell... 

Any comments on this scheme? What are the technical and scientific
problems? How big resources would be needed to resurrect a few
individuals? 

