From: EdRegis@aol.com
Date: Tue, 22 Nov 1994 12:03:15 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-327] NANOTECH: Drexler predicts when it will happen

Nick Szabo (Tue, Nov 22, 1994 1:42 AM EST) writes:
>Nor has Drexler, or anybody else, ever presented a
>sufficiently detailed design for a self-reproducing 
>machine.  The devil is in the last 1% of the details.  
>Drexler assemblers need to be 99.99% self-reproducing 
>or better to be economical, and much better still to perform
>some of the "exponential growth" feats postulated for them. 

Where did this 99.99% figure come from?  The fact is that Drexler has long
since abandoned the notion that self-replication is necessary for molecular
manufacturing. (You won't find much about self-replication in Nanosystems.)
What's important is massive parallelism, not the ability to self-replicate.
 If one assembler can be built through Brownian assembly (nee
"self-assembly"), then billions of them can be built that way in parallel.
 Then, so long as these billions of parallel assemblers can be made to work
in concert, then molecular manufacturing can proceed, and it can proceed
economically.  Neither exponential growth nor self-replication is a necessary
condition of nanotechnology.

>We will, however, see continued, perhaps breathtaking
>progress in lithography, etc., etc., ...

Apparently, some people love to make predictions.  After having written two
books on "futuristic" themes (one of them being nanotechnology), one thing I
have learned is the futility of making predictions about anything not covered
by the laws of the hard sciences.  Quite probably, most of the predictions
that have ever been made have been wrong, and I myself avoid them like the
plague.  

