From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 22 Nov 94 12:08:04 EST
Subject: [#94-11-328] Millenial Fever

Nick Szabo wrote 11/22/94: <I consider the Foresight millenial fever to be such
a tragic piece of news, because I have considered the work done by Drexler,
Merkle et. al. on theoretical applied science to be quite important.  Indeed it
is perhaps the cornerstone of Extropian thought, as it gives us a rational basis
for determining the probability... [for various good things]>

Specific technologies or narrow scientific fields cannot be cornerstones of
philosophic thought.  They can be for a business or an industry, but not a
philosophy.  Extropian thought has, as one cornerstone, our self-reliance on our
rational application of the scientific method coupled to a free market approach
to technological development building on basic science, and above all, our
creativity and ingenuity arising strictly within individual minds that direct us
to overcome current limitations of achieving whatever desires we generate.
Progress is always two steps forward, one step back.  Millenial fevers, like
clockwork, always reach a pitch and break.  Take two aspirin and call me in
2001.

